# Registry server

This project can publish its remotes through a self-hosted mfeflow registry server. The server keeps the pointer
(which version each environment serves) in SQLite, checks every deploy, and takes tokens with narrow scopes. The
build files still live on a static host or bucket; the server only moves the pointer.

## Configuration

- `serverUrl`: <%= it.options.serverUrl === '' ? 'not set yet' : '`' + it.options.serverUrl + '`' %>

This is the address `registry-server` was added with. Commands read it from `MFEFLOW_REGISTRY_URL`, or `--registry-url`.
The token is read only from `MFEFLOW_REGISTRY_TOKEN`; there is no flag for it, so it never lands in your shell history.
Plain http works only for localhost.

## Point the shell at the server

In `public/config/runtime-config.prod.json`, set the index address and the origins it may be on:

```json
{
  "registryUrls": ["https://registry.example.com/v1/index/prod", "/registry/index.json"],
  "registryOrigins": ["https://registry.example.com"],
  "registryBeacon": true
}
```

The second URL is the static fallback the server republishes, so shells still boot if the server is down. Then use
`src/registry-sources.ts` in `src/bootstrap.tsx`: replace `registrySources()` with the one from `./registry-sources`.
`registryBeacon: true` lets browsers report load errors, which is how the server halts a bad percentage rollout.

## Commands

```sh
export MFEFLOW_REGISTRY_URL=https://registry.example.com
export MFEFLOW_REGISTRY_TOKEN=...            # from `mfeflow-registry token create` on the server
pnpm server:deploy catalog --env prod        # upload the build, ask the server to point prod at it
pnpm server:status --env prod                # what the server serves
pnpm server:rollback catalog --env prod      # back to the previous version
pnpm mfeflow kill catalog --env prod         # out of the index at once
pnpm mfeflow deploy catalog --env prod --percent 10 --steps 50,100
pnpm mfeflow rollout status catalog --env prod
```

`deploy` uploads the build to the artifact host first (`--host`, or `registry.host` in `mfeflow.json`), so the host
credentials are the same as for the static registry. Then it asks the server, which reads the files back and runs its
own checks.

## In CI

With `ci-github` in the project, `.github/workflows/deploy-server.yml` deploys every remote when `main` changes. Add
`MFEFLOW_REGISTRY_TOKEN` and the artifact host credentials as secrets of the `registry` environment, and
`MFEFLOW_REGISTRY_URL` as a variable. Give the token only `read` and `deploy` for the environment it deploys to.
